sum='The statue of the Happy Prince stood high above the city, covered with gold leaf, with sapphire eyes and a ruby on his sword-hilt. A little swallow, on his way to Egypt, rested between his feet and found that the prince was crying. When alive the prince lived in the Palace of Sans-Souci, where sorrow could not enter, so the courtiers called him happy; now from the high column he saw all the ugliness and misery of the city. He asked the swallow to take the ruby to a poor seamstress whose son lay ill with fever; the swallow fanned the boy with its wings. Next he sent one sapphire eye to a young playwright who was too cold and hungry to finish a play, and the other to a little match-girl whose matches had fallen into the gutter. Now blind, the prince asked the swallow to go to Egypt, but the swallow stayed out of love and became his eyes. Leaf by leaf the swallow gave away the fine gold to the poor. Winter came; the swallow, dying of cold, kissed the prince on the lips and fell dead; the prince’s leaden heart snapped in two. The Mayor pulled down the shabby statue and melted it, but the broken lead heart would not melt and was thrown on a dust heap with the dead bird. God asked an angel for the two most precious things in the city, and the angel brought the leaden heart and the dead bird.',
